## 1. The Search for N/4

The WIN Paradigm assigns 14 particle modes at eigenvalue λ = 4 on an 8×8
torus to the 14 Standard Model particles. Each mode carries an N/4 value
that orders the particles by mass. Earlier versions of the framework
attempted to derive the N/4 values from the mode structure using a
product formula:

    N/4 = (kL/(H+d)) · PR · exp(−(π/d) · shell)

with PR the participation ratio of the mode vector and shell a fitted
layer index. The framework's own later documentation states that the
shell values are free parameters — one per particle — so the formula
has 14 free parameters in total.

The present paper reports the results of an exhaustive attempt to remove
those free parameters and derive N/4 from first principles. Six independent
approaches were tested:

| Approach | Result |
|:---|:---|
| Product formula N/4 = A · PR · exp(−B · shell) | Implied A varies 6.5× across particles |
| Entropy ordering N/4 = f(S) | S takes 3 values; N/4 has 14 |
| Winding depth N/4 = A · exp(−B · τ(n₁,n₂)) | R² < 0.20; p > 0.05 |
| Bogoliubov–de Gennes quasiparticle spectrum | ≤ 3 distinct eigenvalues on the 14-mode orbit |
| Sum-of-products with framework coefficients | Best fit needs non-framework coefficients |
| Charge-based partition N/4 = f(Q) | R² < 0.08 |

None succeeded. The structural reason is that the 14 modes at λ = 4 form a
single D₄ orbit of the torus symmetry group: any function of a single mode's
properties — including its vector, entropy, participation ratio, winding,
and gap function — is invariant on the orbit and collapses to a handful
of values, whereas N/4 requires 14.

We conclude that the N/4 values are **not derivable from the substrate
geometry**. They are empirical inputs, as particle masses are in every
known framework.

