## 4. Interpretation

In quantum field theory, the vacuum energy density is computed as a sum
over all field modes weighted by their zero-point energies. The sum
diverges at the Planck scale, and the observed value is roughly 10¹²⁰
times smaller than the naive result. This is the cosmological constant
problem. The standard explanation is that an unknown cancellation
mechanism — perhaps supersymmetry, perhaps something else — operates
at high energies.

The WIN Paradigm provides a **discrete, geometric cancellation
mechanism**: the mirror symmetry λ ↔ 8 − λ of the 8×8 torus pairs every
mode with a mirror partner of equal multiplicity, and their zero-point
contributions cancel. The only modes that survive are those at the fixed
point of the mirror map, λ = 4.

**The 14 particles of the Standard Model are the residual of this
cancellation.** They are the modes that cannot be paired, because they
are their own mirrors.

This is the framework's central structural claim, and it is verified
directly from the torus spectrum. It requires no fitted parameters.

